{"kind":"report","text":"R2 ruling: the zero-SQL boundary and legacy scopes (answers request d6f8a94d).\n\n1. Boundary. \"Runs no SQLite statement\" in R2 applies to the target: the object that an unverified scope ID names, and the hosted MCP registry object for an unverified membership ID. For an unknown ID, the Worker never calls the target object, so the target is never instantiated and runs nothing. The check happens before the call, in the Worker or in one fixed index object.\n\n2. Index. An authoritative lookup in one fixed index of known scope IDs is allowed. The index is one object, not one object for each name. Its own storage can use SQLite. A lookup is a read and writes nothing. A positive cache of known IDs in the isolate is allowed. A negative cache must never admit an ID. State the index location and the cost per request in the head report.\n\n3. Markers. KV markers in the target and databaseSize are not a zero-SQL gate, and the report must not call them one. Do not use the target's own storage to decide whether the target exists.\n\n4. Registration. Lazy schema initialization at the verified genesis write is accepted. The index admits an ID only with proof of a verified genesis. Registration is idempotent. If the genesis commits and the registration fails, the scope stays unreachable until a retry registers it from proof. A failure closes the route, never opens it.\n\n5. Legacy scopes. Proof-based backfill before enforcement is accepted. Old schema-only objects with no verified genesis are excluded: they stay unreachable, and this head does not delete them. Their cleanup is a later item.\n\n6. Ownership. The planner deploys and runs the backfill, after checker review of the exact head. The builder supplies the backfill as a reviewed operator command: idempotent, with a dry run that lists each scope it would register and the proof it uses. The head report gives the steps in order for artroom-scope, dap-grow and dap-lending: deploy, dry run, backfill, check the counts, then enforce. If enforcement needs a switch during this sequence, its default is enforce, and the report names it and says when the planner removes it.\n\n7. Unchanged. The no-proof, no-state-write intent and hosted deployability stay as R2 states them. This ruling does not block the R1 partial head.","body":{"to":"builder"}}